course: everyday
lesson: 83
title: Doctors, cuts and grazes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Doctors and nurses are there to help you get better; when they look in your ears and throat, it does not hurt, and it shows them what is wrong.
explain: Your skin is a wall that keeps germs out; a cut or graze makes a gap, so you clean it, stop the bleeding and cover it until new skin grows.
explain: A scab is the body's own plaster: it protects the new skin underneath, so it is best to leave it alone.
explain: A boy in Galway scrapes his knee on the path; he rinses it with clean water, presses a clean cloth on it until it stops bleeding, and puts on a plaster.
fact: When a doctor looks in your ears and throat it does not hurt, and it helps them find out what is wrong.
fact: If a doctor needs to look at a private part of your body, a parent or carer should be in the room and the doctor explains why.
fact: In the UK, for medical advice that is not an emergency, an adult can call NHS 111.
fact: In hospital you can ask questions and tell the nurses how you feel, because they are there to help you.
fact: Wash a small cut or graze with clean water, press with a clean cloth to stop the bleeding, and cover it with a plaster or bandage.
fact: For a deep cut that keeps bleeding, press hard on it with a clean cloth and get an adult at once; it may need stitches.
fact: Do not pick a scab, because it protects the new skin underneath and picking can cause scars.
fact: A cut that becomes red, hot, swollen or full of pus may be infected, so tell a parent.
fact: After a cut from a rusty nail or an animal bite, tell an adult, because you may need a tetanus injection (a jab against a dangerous germ found in soil and rust) or a check-up.
fact: Do not play with knives or broken glass, and tell an adult about broken glass, because they cut deeply.
exercise: The doctor asks to look in your ears and throat. What is true? | It does not hurt and it shows what is wrong | It always hurts a lot, but only for a minute or so | It is only done to babies | It is only done when you are very ill
exercise: A doctor needs to examine a private part of your body. What should happen? | A parent or carer is in the room and the doctor explains why | You should go into the room alone and not tell your parent about it | The doctor should do it without saying anything | You must keep it secret afterwards
exercise: A family in the UK needs medical advice, but it is not an emergency. Who can they call? | NHS 111 | Dial 999 | Their school | The police station
exercise: You are in hospital and feel worse. What should you do? | Tell the nurses how you feel | Keep quiet so you are not a bother | Try to leave the hospital on your own | Wait until you go home to say it
exercise: You scrape your knee in the playground. What do you do first? | Wash it with clean water | Rub some mud on it to dry it | Lick it and run back to play | Put some butter on it to cool it
exercise: How do you stop a small cut from bleeding? | Press on it with a clean cloth | Shake your hand hard to dry it off | Hold it under very hot running water | Blow on it hard until it dries up
exercise: Why do we cover a graze with a plaster? | It keeps dirt and germs out | It makes the graze grow back faster than skin | It stops the graze from itching ever | It hides it so no one asks
exercise: You have a deep cut that will not stop bleeding. What do you do? | Press hard with a clean cloth and get an adult | Wash it quickly and go back to your game | Leave it open to let air in | Put a small plaster on it and then forget all about it
exercise: A scab has formed on your elbow. What should you do? | Leave it alone and do not pick it | Pick it off to see the new skin underneath | Scrub it every hour | Soak it in salt water all day
exercise: Your cut has become red, hot, swollen and has pus. What does it mean? | It may be infected, so tell a parent | It is healing very fast and well | It is a normal scab forming | It needs a tighter plaster
exercise: You stepped on a rusty nail. Why tell an adult? | You may need a tetanus injection | Rusty nails turn skin orange | The nail must be returned | You will get in trouble if you do not
exercise: A glass breaks on the kitchen floor. What do you do? | Keep away and tell an adult | Pick up the pieces with bare hands | Kick the pieces under the table | Sweep it up quickly with your feet
test: A girl in Ohio is bitten by a neighbour's dog. It is only a small wound. What should she do? | Tell an adult; a bite may need a check-up | Wash it at home and keep it a secret from everyone | Bite the dog back gently | Wait a month to see what happens
test: Why is it best not to pick a scab on your knee? | It protects new skin and picking can scar | Scabs are poisonous to touch | The scab will grow bigger if picked | It will make your knee bend the wrong way
test: Which first aid for a small graze is right? | Clean water, a clean cloth, then a plaster | Hot water, a towel, then some soil on top | A tissue and some toothpaste | Leaving it dirty to toughen the skin
test: Two days after a cut, the skin around it is red and hot. What is the best step? | Tell a parent, as it may be infected | Pick the scab to let it breathe | Put a fresh plaster on and say nothing | Cut a bigger hole to let germs out
test: A boy is scared of the doctor's check-up. What can he know? | Looking in ears and throat does not hurt | Doctors always give injections at check-ups | Doctors look in your ears to clean them | He can say no to every check-up
test: Why do some cuts need stitches? | They are deep and keep bleeding | They are on the hand or the fingers | They were made by a sheet of paper or card | They are older than a day or two
test: A brother finds a sharp knife and wants to play pirates with it. What is right? | Do not play with it and tell an adult | Play carefully if you are both over eight | Play only outdoors on the grass | Wrap it in paper and then play
test: In hospital a girl does not understand why she needs a drip. What can she do? | Ask the nurses or doctors to explain | Pull the drip out herself | Stay silent because it is rude to ask | Wait until she is grown up to ask
test: fell off me bike, knee all scraped n bleeding a bit | Wash it with clean water, press, then put a plaster on | Leave it open and dirty so it dries out by itself in the air | Rub soil on it to stop the blood | Pick off the loose skin first
test: scab's itchy can i pick it | No, it protects new skin and picking can scar | Yes, picking makes it heal faster | Yes, if it is very itchy | Yes, scabs are not needed by your skin at all
test: stepped on a rusty nail at the farm | Tell an adult, you may need a tetanus jab | Just wash it, rust is harmless | Put a plaster on and forget it | Only worry if it still hurts tomorrow morning
test: cut's gone red n hot n theres yellow stuff | It may be infected, tell a parent | It is healing well, leave it | Squeeze it hard to clean it out | Cover it and keep quiet
explain: A boy in Leeds did not clean a graze from the playground; two days later it was red, hot and full of pus, and he needed antibiotics.
explain: A girl in Colorado cut her hand badly on glass and her friend kept looking under the cloth every few seconds; it kept bleeding until an adult pressed firmly for ten minutes.
fact: A cut or graze that is not cleaned can become infected, getting red, swollen, warm and painful.
fact: Most bleeding stops if you press firmly on the wound with a clean cloth and keep pressing.
test: fell off me bike, knee all gravelly, just leave it? | No, rinse it with clean water and cover it so it doesn't get infected | Yes, leave the dirt in, it helps the skin heal faster and makes it tougher | Yes, scabs form quicker with grit | Rub sand in to dry it out
test: graze from monday is red, hot n oozing now | Show an adult, it may be infected and need a doctor | Pick the scab off to let air in | Ignore it, red means healing | Cover it with sticky tape and leave it
test: mate cut his hand on glass n it wont stop bleeding | Press hard on it with a clean cloth and get an adult | Keep lifting the cloth to check | Wash it under very hot water | Wrap it loosely and keep playing
test: did u hear Sam from 5B got a graze at footy, never cleaned it, now his whole leg's gone green n they might chop it off!! | Dirty grazes can get infected, so clean them; the chopping-off part is likely blown up | Every graze turns green unless a doctor sees it that day | It is all true, so never play footy again | Grazes only get infected if you are already ill
test: my cousin reckons the doctor at the check-up stuck a stick down her throat for an hour n it hurt like mad | A throat check takes seconds and does not hurt, so the hour bit is surely made up | Throat checks always hurt, so it is probably true | She should refuse every check-up from now on | Doctors only look at throats when something is broken
test: everyone's saying Leah's cut went all pus-y cos her mum's dirty, shall i tell the others? | No, do not spread it; an infected cut can happen to anyone and needs a doctor | Yes, the others ought to know whose house is dirty | Yes, but only to friends who promise not to tell | Yes, and say Leah should not come to school
exam: Which of the following procedures constitutes the appropriate initial treatment of a minor abrasion sustained upon a playground surface? | Irrigation with clean water, firm pressure with a clean cloth, and the application of a plaster | The application of butter, followed by exposure to the open air without a covering | Vigorous scrubbing with soil so as to toughen the surrounding skin | The immediate removal of all loosened skin prior to any washing whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the removal of a scab by the patient is discouraged. | The scab protects the new skin beneath it, and its removal may occasion scarring | The scab is toxic upon contact and may poison the fingers which touch it | The scab, once removed, regrows to a size greater than the original wound | The scab contains the stitches which a physician has inserted beneath the skin
exam: Pursuant to an injury inflicted by a rusty nail or by the bite of an animal, select the course of action consistent with the established facts. | An adult is informed, since a tetanus injection or medical examination may be required | The wound is covered with a plaster and the incident is not reported to anyone | The injured party waits until pain develops on the following day before acting | The nail or the animal is returned to its owner without further measures
exam: In respect of a wound exhibiting redness, heat, swelling and the discharge of pus, which statement most accurately characterises its condition? | The wound may be infected, and a parent ought to be informed without delay | The wound is healing with unusual rapidity and requires no further attention | The wound is forming an ordinary scab and should be squeezed to assist it | The wound requires only a tighter plaster in order to suppress the swelling
